Turning a check into a test
Tests live in tests/testthat/ and run with:
Rscript -e 'testthat::test_dir("tests/testthat")'
test_that("authoritarianism is a 0-1 mean of four items", {
  wss &lt;- readRDS("data/wss_clean.rds")
  expect_true(all(wss$authoritarianism &gt;= 0 &
                  wss$authoritarianism &lt;= 1, na.rm = TRUE))
})

test_that("trump_vote is coded only for two-party voters", {
  wss &lt;- readRDS("data/wss_clean.rds")
  expect_setequal(unique(na.omit(wss$trump_vote)), c(0, 1))
})

A good use of an agent “Read R/01_recode.R and write a testthat test for every derived variable: range, allowed values, and row count. Do not change the recode.” Then read the tests — they are far easier to check than the recode itself, and they will outlive this workshop.

Make the Agent Prove It

test_that("authoritarianism is a 0-1 mean of four items", {
  wss &lt;- readRDS("data/wss_clean.rds")
  expect_true(all(wss$authoritarianism &gt;= 0 & wss$authoritarianism &lt;= 1, na.rm = TRUE))
  expect_equal(nrow(wss), 7600)
})

test_that("trump_vote is coded only for two-party voters", {
  wss &lt;- readRDS("data/wss_clean.rds")
  expect_setequal(unique(na.omit(wss$trump_vote)), c(0, 1))
  expect_equal(sum(!is.na(wss$trump_vote)), 6495)
})


A test is how you make a verification permanent. Write one for every recode you accept from an agent: it costs a minute now and catches the silent change six weeks from now, when somebody “improves” the recode.
